==English==
{{was wotd|2007|August|2}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)mer- (allot)}}
From {{uder|en|la|meretrīcius}}, from {{m|la|meretrīx||harlot, prostitute}}, from {{m|la|mereō||earn, deserve, merit}} (English {{m|en|merit}}) + {{m|la|-trīx||(female agent)}} (English {{m|en|-trix}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌmɛɹɪˈtɹɪʃəs/|/ˌmɛɹəˈtɹɪʃəs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-meretricious.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʃəs|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[tastelessly|Tastelessly]] [[gaudy]]; superficially attractive but having in reality no value or substance; falsely alluring.
#: {{syn|en|specious}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=He looked round the poor room, at the distempered walls, and the bad engravings in '''meretricious''' frames, the crinkly paper and wax flowers on the chiffonier; and he thought of a room like Father Bryan's, with panelling, with cut glass, with tulips in silver pots, such a room as he had hoped to have for his own.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author={{w|Clive James}}|title=North Face of Soho|page=164|publisher=Picador|year_published=2007
|passage=When I lifted my eyes from the page, there was none of the '''meretricious''' argument London always offers that the sole real purpose in life is to hustle for a buck.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|chapter=1|title={{w|Merchants of Doubt}}|author=Naomi Oreskes; Erik M. Conway|passage=Murrow's later death from lung cancer was both tragic and ironic, for during World War II Murrow had been an articulate opponent of '''meretricious''' balance in reporting.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} Involving unlawful sexual connection or lack of consent by at least one party (said of a romantic relationship).
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Of, or relating to [[prostitute]]s or [[prostitution]].

